{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1396718-proposal-for-narrative-rules-lighter-cod-suppliment","id":1396718,"title":"Proposal for Narrative, Rules-Lighter CoD Suppliment","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-06-15T13:25:36-07:00","id":1396718,"title":"Proposal for Narrative, Rules-Lighter CoD Suppliment","content":"Matthew Dawkins suggested that I post this here. I'm sure most of the people here are good at mastering crunchy systems with lots of rule exceptions and things to keep track of, so I'm not sure if this is exactly the right audience.<br /> <br /> Given the (1) excitement for Exalted Essence and Storypath system; (2) innovation in RPG rules technology; and (3) the upcoming Contagion chronicles, I'd like to suggest that Onyx Path offer a mechanically lighter alternative to Chronicles of Darkness for Chronicles of Darkness. This would allow more people to enjoy the CoD setting and themes and make it easier to do crossovers.<br /> <br /> The rules technology for creating such a system already exist (PbtA, Questworlds, Cortex Prime, Fate, Storypath). Some of the CoD quickstarts, the Mirrors / Chronicles books, and simplifications for Hunter and spirits antagonists hint at how such a thing could be done w/in the existing rules framework.<br /> <br /> Thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5685-archivist","name":"Archivist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5685_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-16T04:35:50-07:00","id":1396848,"title":"","content":"For me CoD is carried on the strength of its amazing flavour and atmosphere, and the kind of stories it encourages you to tell. Not seldomly I find myself playing it <i>despite</i> the ruleset, rather than because of it. I feel that system mastery is often too big a factor in how CoD runs, with some questions my players have essentially having to be answered with &quot;Well, if you combine these merits, get a speciality in this skill, remember to use this, this and this power/feature of your splat, and spend willpower liberally, then it works fine!&quot;<br /> But I don't always want my PCs to need to have such a broad view of so many mechanics to do what they wish to do.<br /> It works, but I feel it could work better at times.<br /> <br /> Some elements of the systems also just feel much too mechanically loaded in general. There's a tendency to systematize everything in great detail.<br /> <br /> (Of course, Storytellers can deal with awkwardness through their own rulings, but that would be true for any rules and even if you used no rules at all.)<br /> <br /> However, I wouldn't want to give up all the good in CoD for a rules-lighter version:<br /> - The high diversity, both between splats and inside of them.<br /> - The strong link between lore and how things play out in-game.<br /> - The personal-horror aspect, which is carried far better in a simulationist than in a narrative game.<br /> <br /> For that second point: a &quot;narrative&quot; system tends to carry the implication that you make decisions more for a character than as a character. The spending of meta-currency like Fate Points, accepting Compels, etc, almost by necessity removes you one step from your character. I feel this would not be in the best interest of CoD. Which, along with low level of mechanical diversity between characters, is why I wouldn't recommend a Fate-like approach for this. (The other systems you mention I have never played, so can't really say there. (Though I've heard of the &quot;moves&quot; of PbtA games, and not sure that'd fit very well, either.)<br /> <br /> So I guess I do want a rules-light version, but not necessarily a narrative version. CoD is best for me when the players don't have a lot of any meta-control over the game world, but solely interact with the world through their character's actions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Homebrew</b><br /> - CtL 2E: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1290961-additional-seeming-benefits-for-contracts-2e?p=1454478#post1454478\" target=\"_blank\">Seeming Benefits for every Contract</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim","name":"Unahim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1292_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1080"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-16T07:52:47-07:00","id":1396867,"title":"","content":"Base CofD can already be played quite rules-light. There are subsystems for combat, investigations (for specific types of investigations; but I digress), chases, social manoeuvring, etc, but they're all <i>optional</i>. The base action/conflict resolution of rolling Attribute+Skill with a fitting penalty or against another dice pool can be used for almost everything.<br /> <br /> If you want a rules-light version of all the various splats rather than just the base system, then that is a whole other beast. Unlike Exalted, CofD's different splats are designed to be their own games. Their powers, weaknesses, quirks, etc, all work widely different to fit different needs. I don't think you can have a rules-light corebook that can describe all of them without sacrificing tons of diversity. They all have special systems that reinforces their base themes, and forcing all of that into a shared system would, by necessity, remove a lot of that.<br /> Exalted: Essence works (well, hopefully, we'll see when it comes out) because all the different splats follow the same base model (both ruleswise and storywise), with their powers structured the same way (many powers even <i>shared</i> by all the splats) and their signature drawbacks also follows a similar structure. <br /> <br /> In short, I'm extremely sceptical to this idea. CofD splats are developed into wildly different directions with wildly different rules systems for their particulars, and a unified system would be extremely wanting for at least some of the splats (especially Deviants) no matter how you do it. In comparison, Exalted splats are all developed from the same mold, with only a few variations in their rules. Streamlining those is a cakewalk, relatively speaking.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-16T10:55:22-07:00","id":1396901,"title":"","content":"Well, there's one big hurdle:<br /> <br /> It's not Onyx Path's decision. It's Paradox's decision. They get final say on what gets made for the CofD.<br /> <br /> I think it would be better to pitch a CofD update of Mirrors or some sort: a guide for hacking the game. Rather than focusing on a &quot;lighter&quot; version (I think the comparisons to Essence and StoryPath are way off in that regard), a more general book on how to modify the system, and perhaps more thorough ST guidance on things like navigating the many optional systems that could apply to a given situation, and deal with things like Condition and Merit bloat, is probably a better direction.<br /> <br /> First, it's an easier pitch to Paradox.<br /> <br /> Second, it's a more useful book to the entire CofD community.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-24T19:31:21-07:00","id":1398691,"title":"","content":"Even rules lighter than it already is? What would that even look like? Is there even a <i>game</i> left at that point?<br /> <br /> The only issue I have with Chronicles of Darkness is how every Merit, Discipline, Ritual, etc. has its own rules and sometimes even its own sub-systems, which can be a bit daunting at times. I think things might be improved on that front. But I really like the rest of the system. The Attributes, Skills, and Traits (Health, Willpower, Defense, etc.) and how they are used to compile dice pools for actions is pretty much perfect the way it is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25286-tomorrow-s-nobody","name":"Tomorrow's Nobody","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25286_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-25T02:09:38-07:00","id":1398741,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tomorrow's Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1396718-proposal-for-narrative-rules-lighter-cod-suppliment?p=1398691#post1398691\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even rules lighter than it already is? What would that even look like? Is there even a <i>game</i> left at that point?<br /> <br /> The only issue I have with Chronicles of Darkness is how every Merit, Discipline, Ritual, etc. has its own rules and sometimes even its own sub-systems, which can be a bit daunting at times. I think things might be improved on that front. But I really like the rest of the system. The Attributes, Skills, and Traits (Health, Willpower, Defense, etc.) and how they are used to compile dice pools for actions is pretty much perfect the way it is.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree that the <i>base</i> is pretty light.<br /> But like you say, there's a ton of subsystems. Handling a chase is an entire subsystem that comes with its own table for modifiers, both to rolls and number of successes needed, that is hard enough to memorize that you'll likely need to look it up each time you wish to use it. And that approach is quite common throughout various other subsystems (many splat-specific) too.<br /> And then as you say, there's a ton of individual merits, powers, etc... It's certainly not &quot;ruels light&quot;!<br /> <br /> I mean, if your argument is &quot;The base attributes and dice resolution are simple, so it's already rules light!&quot; then D&amp;D is rules light too. :P Roll 1d20, add your skill or attack modifier, check if you beat the DC, done! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> But nobody would call D&amp;D rules-light in any serious way. It's all the subsystems and individual spells/classes that make it &quot;rules-heavy&quot;, same applies here.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I doubt many want to sacrifice the merits and such just to make it &quot;lighter&quot;. That's sort of the thing with CoFD, it can be a bit bloated at times, but it has a lot of charm that there's so much diversity. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Heavy Arms is exactly correct in his proposal for the &quot;ideal&quot; approach, I think.<br /> <br /> To make the game lighter, I'd look at the following stuff:<br /> - Optional rules to remove speed. You can replace speed in combat with just a simple zones or range band movement system that slots right in. The approach in Hurt Locker isn't bad, but it sort of keeps speed still around so it doesn't simplify that very much. By removing speed as a factor we're less likely to feel like we must include it in subsystems to &quot;make sense&quot;. Individual splat powers would need to be looked at, of course, but that's easy enough. Let a vampire's Celerity allow them to move one extra zone/range band at 1, 3 and 5 Celerity and call it a day, for instance.<br /> - Alternative rules for the heavier subsystems, such as chases, that don't require looking up a table for diverse modifiers. That shouldn't be too hard to do, we can easily bash something out among ourselves.<br /> - Simply equipment and service bonuses and the like.<br /> - A more unified optional &quot;light merits&quot; system could help with merit bloat. I'd probably introduce &quot;story points&quot; then, and make merits give permission to use a story point to call that merit in effect. So if you have &quot;Allies&quot;, spending a story point on it makes those allies important in the current story. Or if you have &quot;Striking Looks&quot;, you can spend a story point on that to make it important. But all merits would essentially just be descriptors of what their narrative impact is, and the storypoint rules would be universal, with a small list of benefits a story point can give IF you have an appropriate merit to use it with. Each merit would only have 1 rank, and you'd have less of them, maybe 3 at char gen. There wouldn't need to be a list of merits, only rules for what is an appropriate merit. They wouldn't bloat too much since story points would be somewhat limited so more merits give you more options, but not ever increasing stacking modifiers on top of one another that add complexity. It's an interesting option, though I wouldn't let it get too meta, like some other meta currencies in other games can get. I'm not sure I'd use this variant myself, but it would really simplify merits for sure.<br /> - The rest would essentially be splat specific changes.<br /> <br /> Only four bullet points, but several of them do cover a lot (like &quot;simplify subsystems&quot;). Still, it all seems fairly doable. ^^","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Homebrew</b><br /> - CtL 2E: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1290961-additional-seeming-benefits-for-contracts-2e?p=1454478#post1454478\" target=\"_blank\">Seeming Benefits for every Contract</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim","name":"Unahim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1292_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1080"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-25T03:01:42-07:00","id":1398744,"title":"","content":"If you don't want to radically change how Merits basically work, I think the biggest thing is really a matter of a somewhat bad tendency (that well predates the CofD):<br /> <br /> The more mechanically complex a part of the game is, the more hooks there are for the granularity of Merits, and thus there's the temptation to bloat the Merit lists.<br /> <br /> This creates issues beyond the number of Merits. You have both general physical Merits, and then subsystem specific physical Merits. Do I take the general Merit that lets me run faster, or the Chase specific Merit that only helps me outrun people in a Chase. Do I take a general Merit that increases my Brawl pool when using grapples, or do I buy the Fighting Style Merit for grappling.<br /> <br /> And of course, in the end, you want to take all the Merits to get the most bonuses.<br /> <br /> If more of the subsystems integrated standard Merits instead of being a source of new subsystem specific Merits, you cut down on these things considerably.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-25T10:40:10-07:00","id":1398821,"title":"","content":"This seems like what the Storyteller Vault is made for.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-25T14:22:28-07:00","id":1398872,"title":"","content":"I'm far from certain. I would definitely double check the STV rules because porting games to a new system (even if it's supposed to be a light-weight version of the existing rules system) may very well be explicitly forbidden.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-25T15:17:28-07:00","id":1398883,"title":"","content":"As I understand it, you can't use the STV to, say, convert CofD to StoryPath, but you could use it for a supplement for a simplified Conditions system.<br /> <br /> This is from the STV FAQ:<br /> <br /> &quot; Or you can create your own custom rules system variants. Products that created with custom, non-traditional rules systems not previously published by White Wolf or its official partners must be stated as such at the beginning of each product for clarity. &quot;<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-25T16:47:37-07:00","id":1398896,"title":"","content":"I was a bit nervous posting here, because the people here are people that don't find the system heavy and don't need a simplified version. <br /> <br /> And I agree the core system is very straightforward. You could simplify it as they did in mirrors or Storypath with just 3 attributes, but that's about it.<br /> <br /> I think what makes it heavy are all of the subsystems (conditions, additional tracks, exceptions for every power and merit, how different supernaturals work, etc.)<br /> <br /> I think there's a huge market for meaty settings with simplified systems (just see the enthusiasm for all of the PbtA and Forged in the Dark Games).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5685-archivist","name":"Archivist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5685_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-25T18:08:05-07:00","id":1398909,"title":"","content":"There's lots of us that think the CofD could use some streamlining, even if we don't see a need to do radical changes.<br /> <br /> Part of the problem with comparing things to Exalted Essence or StoryPath, is that those two systems are basically taking the even more complicated Storyteller/ing systems down to where the CofD is. Comparing Trinity 2e to CofD 2e puts them at roughly the same levels of complexity. StoryPath's big advantage is that it frontloads a lot of complexity, but Talents, Novas, and Psions all use completely different rules for their powers, Edges are just like Merits, etc.<br /> <br /> This is esp. the case because as you just said, it's not the core system that makes CofD a hefty rule set.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-25T20:10:49-07:00","id":1398937,"title":"","content":"As someone trying to get back into CofD after fooling around with other systems, I do like the idea of streamlining a few things but I agree with other posters here that it\u2019s not the core rules that are difficult. I played nWoD first, DnD 5e way later and I think nWoD\u2019s rules in general were way easier\u2014there\u2019s just a lot of sub-systems and different ways supernaturals work. My biggest wish for streamlining would be some way to make it easier to play multiple types of supernaturals in one game. Not impossible, but daunting to learn all the specifics for each splat. <br /> <br /> I like what Unahim said about CofD being a simulation game versus narrative game. I wouldn\u2019t want to give that up. I don\u2019t think rules adjustments or simplifying rules has to go hand in hand with making it a narrative type game, though?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>[He who fights monsters might take care lest he thereby become a monster. And if you gaze too long into an abyss, the abyss also gazes into you. -Nietzsche]","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26210-kisaragi","name":"kisaragi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26210_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-25T22:54:37-07:00","id":1398948,"title":"","content":"If I found this kind of thing on STV or DTRPG, I'd buy it for the curiosity.<br /> <br /> It's the kind of thing I've thought about taking a run at myself, but never got around to, and at this point of my life, I believe I never will. But I'd buy it and read it and maybe chime in on forums and stuff with opinions.<br /> <br /> I do agree that it's worth taking the shot at streamlining the rules. I'm unconvinced anyone <i>can</i>, which is part of why I'd be intrigued by an attempt. My advice, with a grain of salt, would be to have a vision that's more than just &quot;rules-light CofD;&quot; something more like &quot;slice of life Requiem&quot; or &quot;archaeomancer Mage&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-26T03:30:51-07:00","id":1398998,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Unahim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1396718-proposal-for-narrative-rules-lighter-cod-suppliment?p=1398741#post1398741\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree that the <i>base</i> is pretty light.<br /> But like you say, there's a ton of subsystems. Handling a chase is an entire subsystem that comes with its own table for modifiers, both to rolls and number of successes needed, that is hard enough to memorize that you'll likely need to look it up each time you wish to use it. And that approach is quite common throughout various other subsystems (many splat-specific) too.<br /> And then as you say, there's a ton of individual merits, powers, etc... It's certainly not &quot;ruels light&quot;!<br /> <br /> I mean, if your argument is &quot;The base attributes and dice resolution are simple, so it's already rules light!&quot; then D&amp;D is rules light too. :P Roll 1d20, add your skill or attack modifier, check if you beat the DC, done! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> But nobody would call D&amp;D rules-light in any serious way. It's all the subsystems and individual spells/classes that make it &quot;rules-heavy&quot;, same applies here.</div> </div> </div> </div>The difference between CofD and D&amp;D is that all the subsystems of CofD (the base game; no splats) are 100% completely optional. They can all be reduced down to a single roll. Even the combat system (the only system that is so expected to be used you're instructed to put derived traits specifically for that system on your character sheet) has Down &amp; Dirty as an option. Merits are by far the most complicated, but even they require a literal buy-in because they're not used unless you buy them and use them.<br /> If you don't like the Chase rules, just throw them out and roll a contested Stamina+Athletics with whatever modifiers you think should apply.<br /> The various splats are certainly not rules light, no, but I've already stated why I don't think a supplement for all of them wouldn't work. At best you would need one supplement per gameline, which would still make a lot of rules to juggle for the Contagion Chronicle.<br /> <br /> In D&amp;D (though I wouldn't call the current edition particularly rules heavy compared to the actual giants like Exalted), all the rules (except Feats) are supposed to be used, and basic character progression (Class levels) is based around interacting with all the rules of the game. There are tables everywhere (especially for the GM) and antagonists are not much less complicated than PCs (with the only saving grace being how many pregenerated antagonists are available).<br /> A few things can be done to ease the load; generally by choosing not to interact with a few systems. You could run a party of non-casters and have non-casters as enemies to get rid of the bloated list of spells, for example, but combat is the most involved part and that you can't remove.<br /> <br /> <br /> I do, however, think it would be a good idea to rework Merits (as discussed above) as well as revamp the ephemeral entities rules to be less demanding on the ST having to remember or check what prerequisites are necessary for Manifestations, and which Influence levels can grant them, etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-26T04:56:58-07:00","id":1399023,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tessie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1396718-proposal-for-narrative-rules-lighter-cod-suppliment?p=1398998#post1398998\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The difference between CofD and D&amp;D is that all the subsystems of CofD (the base game; no splats) are 100% completely optional. They can all be reduced down to a single roll. Even the combat system (the only system that is so expected to be used you're instructed to put derived traits specifically for that system on your character sheet) has Down &amp; Dirty as an option. Merits are by far the most complicated, but even they require a literal buy-in because they're not used unless you buy them and use them.<br /> If you don't like the Chase rules, just throw them out and roll a contested Stamina+Athletics with whatever modifiers you think should apply.<br /> The various splats are certainly not rules light, no, but I've already stated why I don't think a supplement for all of them wouldn't work. At best you would need one supplement per gameline, which would still make a lot of rules to juggle for the Contagion Chronicle.<br /> <br /> In D&amp;D (though I wouldn't call the current edition particularly rules heavy compared to the actual giants like Exalted), all the rules (except Feats) are supposed to be used, and basic character progression (Class levels) is based around interacting with all the rules of the game. There are tables everywhere (especially for the GM) and antagonists are not much less complicated than PCs (with the only saving grace being how many pregenerated antagonists are available).<br /> A few things can be done to ease the load; generally by choosing not to interact with a few systems. You could run a party of non-casters and have non-casters as enemies to get rid of the bloated list of spells, for example, but combat is the most involved part and that you can't remove.<br /> <br /> I do, however, think it would be a good idea to rework Merits (as discussed above) as well as revamp the ephemeral entities rules to be less demanding on the ST having to remember or check what prerequisites are necessary for Manifestations, and which Influence levels can grant them, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think we're saying anything different, Tessie! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> You're correct, I should have said that CoFD <b>with</b> all of the optional rules (which I don't think most players and tables treat as optional rules most of the time, but fair is fair!) is quite rules heavy. And several merits only have an effect when you use Social Maneuvering or do a Chase, so the expectation that you use it all certainly seems to be there. ^^<br /> <br /> All those subsystems can be reworked to your needs without breaking anything else, so we certainly agree on that.<br /> I'm not really arguing for an official supplement to be made, I was outlining what we ourselves can do. ^^ I agree it'd be impossible to make a supplement with all the splats involved. :P<br /> <br /> One thing I think we really need a toolbox on which could be official: waaaaay simplified &quot;making NPCs&quot; rules in general! Not just for Entities, but really just in general.<br /> I use some house rules based on something PenDragon posted once I think, to quickly set a rating on an NPC and just set 2-3 values and be done with it. Something official like that we could really use!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Homebrew</b><br /> - CtL 2E: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1290961-additional-seeming-benefits-for-contracts-2e?p=1454478#post1454478\" target=\"_blank\">Seeming Benefits for every Contract</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim","name":"Unahim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1292_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1080"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-26T08:07:38-07:00","id":1399069,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Unahim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1396718-proposal-for-narrative-rules-lighter-cod-suppliment?p=1399023#post1399023\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thing I think we really need a toolbox on which could be official: waaaaay simplified &quot;making NPCs&quot; rules in general! Not just for Entities, but really just in general.<br /> I use some house rules based on something PenDragon posted once I think, to quickly set a rating on an NPC and just set 2-3 values and be done with it. Something official like that we could really use!</div> </div> </div> </div>You mean like Brief Nightmares on page 143 except without Dread Powers?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-01T00:45:36-07:00","id":1400258,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tessie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1396718-proposal-for-narrative-rules-lighter-cod-suppliment?p=1399069#post1399069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You mean like Brief Nightmares on page 143 except without Dread Powers?</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Like&quot;, yes, but not that exactly! ^^<br /> <br /> Brief Horrors for instance assumes the things you're making are, well, horrors, and thus their health pools scale directly with their best pool. And their defense can be quite high too, based on &quot;All Other Pools&quot;, for instance for a great politician...<br /> <br /> So indeed, a system &quot;like&quot; it, but not &quot;it&quot;. ^^<br /> <br /> With some tweaks it might be made to suit.<br /> <br /> I use a modified version of these rules (actually by NeoTiamat, not Pendragon, my bad!): <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/9725-peripheral-npcs-and-combat-minions\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...combat-minions</a><br /> I made rank 0 slightly more competent, made the steps less wide, and updated a bit to current rules and filled in some missing values, like defense. I like that it's slightly more granular (can have characters that are average at everything, or very specialized, and there's more than three &quot;types&quot;).<br /> <br /> In the middle of play it's still quite easy to go &quot;Ok, he's rank 2, so I'll give him X/Y/Z, aand... if need be I can flesh it out as we go.&quot; and there you go. ^^<br /> <br /> It works for me, but something official would be very useful for a lot of people from the talk I see around the web. ^^ And if that simply mans somebody takes the Quick Horrors rules, broadens it to 5-6 ranks and tweaks it slightly, then great! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Homebrew</b><br /> - CtL 2E: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1290961-additional-seeming-benefits-for-contracts-2e?p=1454478#post1454478\" target=\"_blank\">Seeming Benefits for every Contract</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim","name":"Unahim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1292_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1080"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-01T03:12:57-07:00","id":1400266,"title":"","content":"&quot;<i>If</i> you find yourself needing Advantages like Health or Defense, use the following benchmarks as guidelines. As always, <i>adjust as you see fit</i>;[..]&quot;<br /> <br /> Does the politician need Health or Defense? If for some reason yes, then you could just change which pool to add as appropriate. If you want less spread over the three categories I'd recommend using 4 + All Other Pools, which nets you &quot;minions&quot; with below average human Health and &quot;lone terrors&quot; with not quite maxed human Health.<br /> Defense looks completely reasonable to me (Attribute+Skill, just as their average dice pools; might want a -1 to simulate choosing the lower of two Attributes), though I would definitely switch out the dice pool used if I wanted them to either be especially good or bad at defending. I don't know why that isn't used in the book.<br /> <br /> Instead of just skipping Dread Powers you could also add up to that many Merit dots. Would make them stand out more if they have some unique mechanics, but it's certainly not necessary for random bystanders dragged into the plot by the PCs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":11887,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","user":"Tessie","date":". 07-01-2020, 06:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-05T11:35:26-07:00","id":1401156,"title":"","content":"As someone who has spent the past couple of years running relatively rules-light PbtA games (lots of Monsterhearts for instance!), coming back to my old NWoD favourites was like a punch in the face in terms of complexity.<br /> <br /> The base die system is a bit hefty, but not too bad on its own. But all the subsystems really add up - and while some might be tagged as optional, many of the big ones (Combat, Integrity) are not. And once you get into splats... Damn, Vampire has Frenzy, Predatory Auras, Touchstones, Feeding, etc, etc even before you get into things specific to individual characters like powers or merits.<br /> <br /> I think the first step in taking CoD from &quot;rules medium&quot; to &quot;rules light&quot; would be abandoning the general assumption that if something changes how things work in the fiction, it must also have some mechanical impact or a system for it. But that's a big part of the basic assumptions for simulationist games like CoD, and it'd really really change the feel if one did so.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2007-wade-l","name":"Wade L","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2007_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"136"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-05T15:39:25-07:00","id":1401208,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wade L</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1396718-proposal-for-narrative-rules-lighter-cod-suppliment?p=1401156#post1401156\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But all the subsystems really add up - and while some might be tagged as optional, many of the big ones (Combat, Integrity) are not.</div> </div> </div> </div>Integrity isn't optional, but it's hardly a hefty system. Roll Resolve + Composure +/- modifiers, apply the results based on the normal result options. It's slightly more than a page for the description and mechanics of it, and most of that is a table of suggested/sample modifiers. Though different gamelines have different Integrity equivalents which do range a bit in terms of complexity.<br /> <br /> Combat is at least optional in the sense of there are two core combat systems (assuming you even get into combat) to pick from. You can always run a game where all combat is Down &amp; Dirty and then skip over the big full combat rules: just tell your players so they don't invest in stuff that's not going to come up (incidentally also cutting down on Merit bloat a lot).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And once you get into splats... Damn, Vampire has Frenzy, Predatory Auras, Touchstones, Feeding, etc, etc even before you get into things specific to individual characters like powers or merits. </div> </div> </div>Even PbtA games suffer this though via playbooks. Yes, PbtA is a lighter system, but that doesn't stop Witches in Monsterhearts from having a unique game mechanic via Tokens to spend on Hexes; before you get to every character type's unique Moves. And Monsterhearts, while an awesome game, is also a much more narrow game in terms of scope. It can stay as light as it does because it doesn't try to do as much. If you expanded it out to as many different game play scenarios as the CofD covers, you'd see the same creeping up in complexity that adding more playbooks does.<br /> <br /> Regardless of how light the core of the game engine is, and how much the designers want to keep it that way in practice, if you want to have a horde of different supernatural critters, and make them all feel unique in play, you're going to add complexity to the system. If you're going to add more viable playstyles and game scenarios, you're going to add complexity to the system.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think the first step in taking CoD from &quot;rules medium&quot; to &quot;rules light&quot; would be abandoning the general assumption that if something changes how things work in the fiction, it must also have some mechanical impact or a system for it. But that's a big part of the basic assumptions for simulationist games like CoD, and it'd really really change the feel if one did so. </div> </div> </div>This seems... backwards to me. When things change how things work in the fiction is precisely where the focus of the mechanics should be. Again, this is the whole point of having playbooks with unique moves in PbtA games. On archetype does things differently than another, so they get different mechanics to represent that.<br /> <br /> If you want to trim down the rules, it makes the most sense to start with all the rules that don't or redundantly represent state changes; hence things like reducing Merit and Condition bloat as a common refrain. Also things like standardizing mechanics more: if most supernatural shave an &quot;aura&quot; mechanic (Predatory Aura, Hunter Aspect, Flaring Nimbus, etc.) there should be a more standardized mechanic for it where the differences in accessing it are more like different Moves than distinctly different subsystems.<br /> <br /> This really isn't about &quot;narrative vs. simulation&quot; or whatever. It's about how bespoke the design is supposed to be. The more bespoke the gameplay experience, the easier it is to keep the rules light. The more the group is expected to take a more general system and pick and choose what parts they're going to want, the heavier the rules get; esp. if the designers want you to be able to shift the tone of the game over time. If you just want to play Monsterhearts, Monsterhearts is better than CofD cut down to what you need to run Monsterhearts. If you want to start with Monsterhearts, and then transition to a mix of Monster of the Week and Urban Shadows.. well then CofD looks a lot more appealing as is.<br /> <br /> It's easy to talk about how rules light a specific PbtA game is, because a designer has already trimmed things down to a specific and highly focused gameplay experience.<br /> <br /> If you tried to make a &quot;PbtA Core&quot; with everything you need to play Apocalypse World, Monsterhearts, Masks, Urban Shadows, Ruma, the Sprawl, and so on with one book? It would get a lot bigger and a lot more full of &quot;optional&quot; rules as it would grow to at least look like Fate Core or Cortex Prime.<br /> <br /> And idea of making the CofD lighter by making it more bespoke is a bad one. Bespoke games already exist, and are great, and there's no need to remove things some CofD fans like to become more like them to compete for the people already playing them. The CofD core appeal is it's toolkit approach that's in the middle between highly bespoke systems and more generic systems. Pitching more rules light approaches to the CofD need to maintain that part of the brand identity to really have value.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-05T19:50:29-07:00","id":1401257,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1396718-proposal-for-narrative-rules-lighter-cod-suppliment?p=1401208#post1401208\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This really isn't about &quot;narrative vs. simulation&quot; or whatever. It's about how bespoke the design is supposed to be. The more bespoke the gameplay experience, the easier it is to keep the rules light. The more the group is expected to take a more general system and pick and choose what parts they're going to want, the heavier the rules get; esp. if the designers want you to be able to shift the tone of the game over time.</div> </div> </div> </div>I guess this kind of leads towards what I meant about how CoD ties rules to fiction in a simulationist kind of way - the CoD stuff tends to be a pretty big tent that tries to describe, via rules, how a particular type of *world* works. Inside that world, you can run any kind of story you want, but the core rules of CoD aren't really about that - indeed, they can't be, because, again, you can run any kind of story you want.<br /> <br /> PbtA games typically aren't about creating an engine to simulate how the world works, they're about capturing how a particular type of story works. They're all bespoke because a &quot;PbtA Core&quot; would be inherently nonsensical. They all have to be bespoke just because of the nature of the beast.<br /> <br /> So if you want to slim a CoD game down to a similiar type of thing, you either have to a) cut it down to just one small type of story, which loses all the breadth, or b) go with a more generic narrative system where you lose all the cool detail.<br /> <br /> Which, I guess, in the end is kind of just how it goes - if you want a smaller, lighter game you gotta choose what you're sacrificing. It doesn't come for free. There's a reason why, as much as I love my PbtA stuff, I'm coming back to CoD stuff now.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2007-wade-l","name":"Wade L","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2007_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"136"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-06T11:26:24-07:00","id":1401375,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1292_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1292\" data-vbnamecard=\"1292\">Unahim</a> I'm almost positive the core rulebook has a quick box rules and like 10 examples. If you're looking for throw away npcs, the book basically says to come up with a concept, a few rolls that he'd do, and you're golden. Me personally, I have the base for all of them be &quot;roll three or resist two&quot; and then modify as the concept requires. Dealing with a PI? Increase stealth and shoot rolls. High school jock? Increase physical, lower mental. <br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2007-wade-l\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2007_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2007\" data-vbnamecard=\"2007\">Wade L</a> a lot of rolls in the splats are just down to a roll, making it as rules light as can be without removing the mechanics. Frenzy? Res+Com. That chart of modifiers aren't set in stone, but are more a baseline to use. But all such charts say these arent the only things to use, and each character has had different experiences, changing the rolls further. In all my years of STing I don't think I've looked at a modifier chart once during gameplay. Someone lit a match in front of you? +2 because its small and you're used to smokers -3 because he's hungry and you hate this guy. Did I miss some modifiers? Probably, but they atent important to whats happening now. If you pay more attention to the story rather than every little chart in the book, its rules light. And such things are encouraged in the book. And to make things easier on my players, I have a sheet for each splat to let them know what the splat does (frenzy, aura, etc.) And I encourage my players to make their own sheet for powers and merits and whatnot.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/13.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4793-hunterinthenight","name":"HunterInTheNight","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"168"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-06T12:19:43-07:00","id":1401379,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HunterInTheNight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1396718-proposal-for-narrative-rules-lighter-cod-suppliment?p=1401375#post1401375\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">a lot of rolls in the splats are just down to a roll, making it as rules light as can be without removing the mechanics. Frenzy? Res+Com. That chart of modifiers aren't set in stone, but are more a baseline to use.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's just the base resolution mechanism. What about the rules for what constitutes Frenzy triggers? Alternate ways of resisting Frenzy like postponing rolls with Willpower points, or attempting to Ride the Wave? The effects of being in Frenzy such as the +BP modifier on physical rolls, or immunity against being influenced against your base Frenzy target, or the ability to do bite attacks without being in a grapple with your +1L teeth? The possibility to go counter your Frenzy target for a turn by spending WP? The ability of Touchstones to talk you out of Frenzy? And don't forget Conditions that can be granted by succeeding on Frenzy resist rolls and/or that resolve by entering Frenzy.<br /> Those are all rules that belong to the larger Frenzy system. (Though I might've gotten a few of the minor details wrong.)<br /> <br /> All subsystems for all the various splats are filled to the brim with different rules even if the resolution mechanism for all of them are based on the same types of rolls that are used in the CofD core book. That's a huge difference between CofD and the splat games. In CofD every subsystem (except Integrity) can be replaced with a single roll with modifiers determined at the time of the roll. Even <i>combat</i> can be resolved this way; it's called Down &amp; Dirty.<br /> But once you dig into any of the other games you end up with many subsystems designed around aspects of that particular splat, each with their own collection or rules tailored to a particular experience. It's incredibly rare to find a system that is only &quot;roll this dice pool; these are the results&quot;. Rarer still is finding a subsystem in one of those games that is actually designed to be optional. You are not expected to be able to just replace them wholesale with a single roll appropriate for the situation. That's what makes the splat games so very different from base CofD. And because they have their subsystems tailored for each individual game, they're all quite different from each other as well.<br /> <br /> Going from one game to another time and time again like I do, it's very clear that the Storytelling System framework (how to form dice pools, the different kinds of rolls, Conditions, Clash of Wills) is mostly the same across all games, thankfully, but all the games are still very different and very much their own separate games. That became painfully obvious when I started learning Deviant for one game, and now again when I'm delving into Changeling for a soon to be started game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-06T21:15:19-07:00","id":1401425,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HunterInTheNight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1396718-proposal-for-narrative-rules-lighter-cod-suppliment?p=1401375#post1401375\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">[USER=&quot;1292&quot;]<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2007-wade-l\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2007_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2007\" data-vbnamecard=\"2007\">Wade L</a> a lot of rolls in the splats are just down to a roll, making it as rules light as can be without removing the mechanics. Frenzy? Res+Com. That chart of modifiers aren't set in stone, but are more a baseline to use. But all such charts say these arent the only things to use, and each character has had different experiences, changing the rolls further. In all my years of STing I don't think I've looked at a modifier chart once during gameplay. Someone lit a match in front of you? +2 because its small and you're used to smokers -3 because he's hungry and you hate this guy. Did I miss some modifiers? Probably, but they atent important to whats happening now. If you pay more attention to the story rather than every little chart in the book, its rules light. And such things are encouraged in the book. And to make things easier on my players, I have a sheet for each splat to let them know what the splat does (frenzy, aura, etc.) And I encourage my players to make their own sheet for powers and merits and whatnot.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean like... Even something like Frenzy has tons of rules:<br /> * What triggers frenzies<br /> * Resisted by rolling Resolve + Composure<br /> * Dramatic failure means the frenzy cannot end until a breaking point<br /> * Failure means falling to frenzy, success means you don't<br /> * Success grants you the Tempted Condition<br /> * Exceptional success means you regain Willpower<br /> * When you enter Frenzy, gain a Beat<br /> * Willpower can't be spent to gain +3 dice, but to forstall the roll - each turn forstalled gets you +1 die<br /> * Once Frenzy begins, you have to decide what the Beast wants and follow it until the end<br /> * Torpor can end Frenzy<br /> * A touchstone can talk a vampire down with a social roll, requiring an extended roll with a target of 3 times the vampire's Blood Potency<br /> * Add Bloody Potency to any Strength, Dexterity, or Stamina rolls or resistances<br /> * Ignore Wound penalties during frenzy<br /> * Biting and grappling can be done as a single action during frenzy<br /> * Attempts to coerce a character in Frenzy into something contrary to the Beast's desires automatically fails<br /> * Taking any action contrary to the Beast's agenda requires a point of Willpower per turn<br /> * If multiple characters simultaneously use Willpower to resist, the first to give in gets bigger physical bonuses than the others<br /> <br /> That isn't even including Riding the Wave stuff or like the whole definition of how a vampire acts during Frenzy. That is not a rules light set of mechanics.<br /> <br /> A rules light system for Frenzy might be something like &quot;Sometimes Vampires succumb to their feral instincts and enter a state called Frenzy. If provoked, roll Resolve + Composure to resist. Decide between you and your Storyteller what your character does during Frenzy.&quot;<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2007-wade-l","name":"Wade L","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2007_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"136"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-07T20:13:21-07:00","id":1401606,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1396718-proposal-for-narrative-rules-lighter-cod-suppliment?p=1398821#post1398821\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This seems like what the Storyteller Vault is made for.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is very well said!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"> <hr /><b><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Roleplaying</span> not <span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Rollplaying</span> or <span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Ruleplaying</span></span></b></div> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><u><b>Current</b> <b>Focus</b></u><br /> <b><span style=\"color:#800000\">Storypath &amp; Storypath to Run CoD, VtR, WtF, MtA</span></b><br /> <span style=\"color:#008000\"><b>Dungeons &amp; Dragons 5th Edition</b></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25095-graylion","name":"Graylion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25095_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"368"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-12T10:38:21-07:00","id":1402426,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tessie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1396718-proposal-for-narrative-rules-lighter-cod-suppliment?p=1398998#post1398998\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The difference between CofD and D&amp;D is that all the subsystems of CofD (the base game; no splats) are 100% completely optional. They can all be reduced down to a single roll. Even the combat system (the only system that is so expected to be used you're instructed to put derived traits specifically for that system on your character sheet) has Down &amp; Dirty as an option. Merits are by far the most complicated, but even they require a literal buy-in because they're not used unless you buy them and use them.<br /> If you don't like the Chase rules, just throw them out and roll a contested Stamina+Athletics with whatever modifiers you think should apply.<br /> The various splats are certainly not rules light, no, but I've already stated why I don't think a supplement for all of them wouldn't work. At best you would need one supplement per gameline, which would still make a lot of rules to juggle for the Contagion Chronicle.<br /> <br /> In D&amp;D (though I wouldn't call the current edition particularly rules heavy compared to the actual giants like Exalted), all the rules (except Feats) are supposed to be used, and basic character progression (Class levels) is based around interacting with all the rules of the game. There are tables everywhere (especially for the GM) and antagonists are not much less complicated than PCs (with the only saving grace being how many pregenerated antagonists are available).<br /> A few things can be done to ease the load; generally by choosing not to interact with a few systems. You could run a party of non-casters and have non-casters as enemies to get rid of the bloated list of spells, for example, but combat is the most involved part and that you can't remove.<br /> <br /> <br /> I do, however, think it would be a good idea to rework Merits (as discussed above) as well as revamp the ephemeral entities rules to be less demanding on the ST having to remember or check what prerequisites are necessary for Manifestations, and which Influence levels can grant them, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> You're probably right, however you have deep system mastery so it's obvious to you how to do that. But lets say I look at Demon (which is amazing) and I try to run it. The number of powers and subsystems and their interactions is very high. I'm sure many of them could be simplified, but i'm not sure where to start.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5685-archivist","name":"Archivist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5685_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-12T10:47:27-07:00","id":1402427,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Archivist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1396718-proposal-for-narrative-rules-lighter-cod-suppliment?p=1402426#post1402426\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're probably right, however you have deep system mastery so it's obvious to you how to do that. But lets say I look at Demon (which is amazing) and I try to run it. The number of powers and subsystems and their interactions is very high. I'm sure many of them could be simplified, but i'm not sure where to start.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tessie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1396718-proposal-for-narrative-rules-lighter-cod-suppliment?p=1398998#post1398998\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(the base game; no splats)</div> </div> </div> </div>See my later post about how extremely involved the splat games are.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-12T14:13:40-07:00","id":1402464,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HunterInTheNight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1396718-proposal-for-narrative-rules-lighter-cod-suppliment?p=1401375#post1401375\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1292_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1292\" data-vbnamecard=\"1292\">Unahim</a> I'm almost positive the core rulebook has a quick box rules and like 10 examples. If you're looking for throw away npcs, the book basically says to come up with a concept, a few rolls that he'd do, and you're golden. Me personally, I have the base for all of them be &quot;roll three or resist two&quot; and then modify as the concept requires. Dealing with a PI? Increase stealth and shoot rolls. High school jock? Increase physical, lower mental.</div> </div> </div> </div>As far as I can see, those 10 examples are fully statted out, they have attributes, skills, etc... ^^ There doesn't seem to be a simpler system presented. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I like having a mix of approaches. For totally unexpected encounters, or throw-away ones, obviously you can just slap a dicepool on it and call it a day, without trouble. But I like statting out all NPCs that PCs have contact with on a regular basis in more detail than that. But not so much detail that I can't go through like 10 of them in an hour every so often, yet more detail so that a pattern of &quot;3 dice +/- this&quot; doesn't become so obvious. Inevitably, these will also often be members of the same splat the PCs are part of. (I tend not to mix splats, I find it dilutes the themes of all splats involved a lot.) And sometimes you want to stat out an elder vampire juuuuust in case, but you don't want to spend more than 5 minutes on it, but you want a bit more freedom than slapping 3 fixed dice pools on it.<br /> <br /> I'd say that in terms of rising detail and lowering &quot;speed&quot; of use the steps I use/will use are:<br /> - Just wing a dice pool<br /> - Brief Horror rules with some tweaks (like tessie suggested)<br /> - The peripheral NPC quick rules that I already use, as mentioned in this post<br /> - Fully statting an NPC<br /> <br /> It's that third one that <i>I</i> feel could have a place if an official &quot;toolbox for speeding up your game/planning&quot; if one was ever made. Perhaps not everyone would use that and many are fine with using the first two options on the list. But there also, consider that people with less experience in the system don't necessarily think of using Brief Horrors, and don't necessarily feel confident winging dice pools or tweaking the Brief Horror rules to suit them since they don't know what values make sense or make for good game feel/balance yet. There's this tendency on forums to assume because we--the ones ultra-involved and very knowledgeable of the rules--can cope, that there isn't a hole to be filled (the &quot;Defense and attack dice pools are too closer together!&quot; threads that <i>keep</i> popping up every few months like clockwork are another such thing that clearly bothers many prospective players but not forum veterans, but that's a discussion for another day). But since the only methods available rely on tweaking rules not explicitly made to purpose <i>or</i> require a robust feel for what size of dice pool make sense to begin with, I absolutely feel that there is a hole there.<br /> <br /> It doesn't take more than a minute of googling to find that there are clearly people struggling with this, and not all of them will find their way to the forums before just shrugging and throwing the system in the bin for &quot;too much prepwork&quot;:<br /> - <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/WhiteWolfRPG/comments/6qb8dd/cofd_quick_npc_creation/\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.reddit.com/r/WhiteWolfRP..._npc_creation/</a><br /> - <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/WhiteWolfRPG/comments/6o22um/rules_for_quick_npc_creation/\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.reddit.com/r/WhiteWolfRP..._npc_creation/</a><br /> - <a href=\"https://rpg.stackexchange.com/questions/41837/how-to-stat-up-and-judge-the-challenge-of-supernatural-npcs\" target=\"_blank\">https://rpg.stackexchange.com/questi...ernatural-npcs</a> (preCTL 2e question, but note the author's tags)<br /> <br /> It's clearly something people who aren't steeped in the mechanics consider a problem, so I don't think it'd be bad if a hypothetical supplement would address it. ^^<br /> <br /> Just my two cents! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Homebrew</b><br /> - CtL 2E: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1290961-additional-seeming-benefits-for-contracts-2e?p=1454478#post1454478\" target=\"_blank\">Seeming Benefits for every Contract</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim","name":"Unahim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1292_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1080"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}